### This PR

Tree-based indexes like the ART are typically not designed with bulk operations in mind. Instead of focusing on bulk operations, operations center on tuple-at-a-time execution. Thus, in the context of bulk changes, the ART suffers from the problem of many small allocations. Inserting, updating, or deleting a node in an ART always only affects a small memory region. Leaves are often lightweight, i.e., they also cause many tiny allocations.

Because nodes are persistent beyond a specific ART operation, managing their memory requires a memory manager. This memory manager keeps track of freed sections of memory (deletions) and reclaims them when creating new nodes (insertions). Keeping track of differently sized memory sections for the different node sizes increases the memory manager's complexity; therefore, utilizing different memory managers for fixed-size allocations for the different nodes is more beneficial.

This PR adds the `FixedSizeAllocator` class. A fixed-size allocator allocates `Storage::BLOCK_ALLOC_SIZE` buffers of memory. Each buffer allows for a set number of fixed-size allocations corresponding to the different ART node types and contains a bit mask to track its free positions. Thus, the `FixedSizeAllocator` provides pointers to fixed-size sections of pre-allocated memory. The ART now contains a `FixedSizeAllocator` for each node type. To allow for not-inlined leaves (more than one row ID) and prefixes (exceeding 8 bytes), there are two additional fixed-size allocators (`leaf_segments` and `prefix_segments`). This PR also fully reworks the different node classes and many iterator methods.

ART nodes are no longer pointers to tiny allocated memory sections. Instead, the `ARTNode` inherits from the `SwizzleablePointer` class. The `SwizzleablePointer` contains 8 bytes, of which one bit encodes the swizzle flag, 7 bits encode the ART node type, 3 bytes store the offset, and 4 bytes store the buffer ID (not swizzled) or block ID (swizzled). This is done using C++’s bit fields. With these pointers, it is possible to find a child node either by retrieving data from the respective buffer or lazily loading it from storage.

Due to this new way of managing the memory of the ART, a vacuum operation might become necessary. That is because instead of freeing tiny memory sections during deletes, we now make them available again in the bit mask of their buffer, i.e., no memory is freed. We might call a vacuum to free some excess memory during bulk deletions or when aborting a bulk append due to a constraint violation.

### Future steps

This PR is part of three PRs planned to make the ART buffer managed and less memory-intensive, ultimately allowing us to offload some of its sections. The following subsequent PRs are planned.

1. In this PR, the `FixedSizeAllocator` allocates its buffers. Instead, these should be `BufferHandles` managed by duckdb's buffer manager.
2. Currently, each node contains a prefix. However, prefixes are often zero, creating a big memory overhead and making inlining row IDs into leaf pointers impossible. To overcome this problem, we will add an additional node type, `Node1`, which can be chained to contain all prefix bytes. This will also help with increasing performance for insertions, because most of the overhead there currently happens due to calling `GetPrefix` for each node, even if its prefix is empty.
```cpp
class Node1 {
   uint8_t count;
   data_t prefix[7];
   ARTPointer child;
};
```

